"executive_summary": "This study investigates the acute toxicity of six engineered nanoparticles (Ag, Au, CuO, ZnO, TiO2, SiO2) and three soluble salts on the hatching and early development of Artemia sp. Given the increasing environmental release of nanomaterials, a simple and efficient test to estimate acute toxicity is required. The authors utilized Artemia cysts and nauplii as model organisms due to their robustness and cost-effectiveness. The study employed two parallel assays: a hatching rate test using hydrated and bleached cysts, and a standard Artemia toxicity test monitoring nauplii viability. Physicochemical characterization via TEM and DLS revealed that nanoparticles exhibited different behaviors in salt water, with some showing flocculation. The results demonstrated a clear order of toxicity: Ag > CuO > ZnO > Au > TiO2 > SiO2. Silver and copper oxide nanoparticles showed the highest toxicity, significantly reducing hatching rates and increasing larval mortality. A strong inverse correlation was found between hatching rates and larval mortality, suggesting that the hatching test can serve as a valid pre-screening tool for nanoparticle hazard assessment. The study also observed oxidative stress markers (ROS) in exposed nauplii, particularly with silver nanoparticles. The findings highlight the utility of Artemia assays for high-throughput screening of nanomaterial toxicity in aquatic environments.",
